// Runtime/ClockRuntime.maxon — the two host clocks that are NOT the scheduler's: the CALENDAR
// (`__clock_now_unix_s`) and this THREAD's own CPU consumption (`__thread_cpu_ticks`).
//
// ⭐ WHY THIS IS ITS OWN FILE AND NOT A FUNCTION IN `GtRuntime`. `__gt_now_ns` lives there because the
// green-thread scheduler OWNS it: the timer heap, the sleep deadline and the netpoll wait are all
// expressed in its monotonic nanoseconds, so the clock and its consumer are one mechanism. A wall clock
// has no such relationship — nothing in the scheduler can use a reading that steps backwards when NTP
// corrects a drift, which is precisely what a calendar time does. Filing it under `__gt_` would say the
// scheduler owns it, and `isGtRuntimeCallee` would then install the WHOLE scheduler for a program whose
// only clock question is "what is today's date".
//
// So it wears its own `__clock_` band, gets its own `RuntimeUsage` bit, and installs one function and one
// `.data` word. The MONOTONIC pair (`__Builtins.currentTimeNanos` / `currentTimeMs`) deliberately does NOT
// move here: it routes to the scheduler's existing `__gt_now_ns`, because that reading and the scheduler's
// deadlines must come from ONE counter — two monotonic clocks that could disagree is the bug the split
// identity in `buildGtNowNs` exists to avoid.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND THE FOURTH CLOCK, WHICH IS THE ONLY ONE THAT IS NOT A CLOCK — `__thread_cpu_ticks`.** The three
// above all measure WALL time, so a duration taken with any of them counts every OTHER process on the box:
// a compiler phase timed on a busy machine reports the machine. This one advances only while the CALLING
// THREAD is scheduled, so it cannot see preemption at all, and that is precisely what makes `PhaseProbe`'s
// per-phase cost survive a loaded host — `docs/optimization-log.md`'s `## CPU` table is what it feeds.
//
// ⚠ **ITS UNIT IS PLATFORM-DEFINED AND THERE IS NO HONEST CONVERSION**: TSC ticks on Windows
// (`QueryThreadCycleTime`), nanoseconds under POSIX (`clock_gettime(CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID)`).
// `QueryPerformanceFrequency` is the PERFORMANCE COUNTER's rate and not the TSC's, so a normalization here
// would be a guess wearing a unit's name. The entry point says `ticks`; callers compare RATIOS, which are
// unit-free, or absolutes within one platform.
//
// ⭐ **IT SHARES THIS FILE WITH THE WALL CLOCK AND WEARS A SECOND `__` BAND, WHICH IS `CpuParallelRuntime`'s
// SHAPE FOR A DIFFERENT REASON.** There, one file wears two bands because its two entries answer *may this
// target run it* differently. Here they answer it the SAME way — both are x64-windows-only Win32 reads,
// both refused elsewhere by `calleeNeedsWin32Substrate` — and the split is about COST instead: they reach
// two different imports, and the optional trailing import band is gated per producer. One shared bit would
// make a program that reads the calendar import `QueryThreadCycleTime`, and a program that measures its own
// cost import `GetSystemTimeAsFileTime`. Same file, because the two are the same MECHANISM: a host read
// whose answer arrives through an out-param scratch word — a FRAME slot since W212, and the reason that
// changed is `ClockScratchSlot`'s.

// The prefix every wall-clock runtime entry point shares — the "does this program read the CALENDAR?" test.
let ClockRuntimeCallPrefix = b"__clock_"

export let WallClockNowUnixSecondsName = b"__clock_now_unix_s"

// The `__thread_` band — the "does this program ask how much CPU TIME its own thread has used?" test, and
// the second thing `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate` reads out of this file for E3104. A PREFIX
// rather than a name compare for `isProcessRuntimeCallee`'s reason: an entry point added to this band later
// is gated by construction rather than by memory.
//
// ⚠ **DELIBERATELY NOT `__cpu_`.** That band is `isCpuCountRuntimeCallee`, and it drives `usesCpuCount` —
// the sole producer of `GetActiveProcessorCount` and of the optional trailing import band it turns on.
// Filing this entry there would make every program that times a phase import an API it never calls, and
// install `__cpu_count`'s body beside it. The two questions are unrelated besides: one asks the MACHINE how
// many processors exist, the other asks the host how long THIS thread has run.
let ThreadCpuRuntimeCallPrefix = b"__thread_"

export let ThreadCpuTicksName = b"__thread_cpu_ticks"

// ⭐⭐ **THE OUT-PARAM SCRATCH IS A FRAME SLOT, NOT A `.data` WORD (W212) — and that is a MULTI-M FIX rather
// than tidiness.** Both host reads here take a pointer to write their answer into, and both used to point at
// a shared global: `GetSystemTimeAsFileTime` into `__clock_wall_ticks`, `QueryThreadCycleTime` into
// `__thread_cpu_ticks_out`. The safety argument at those words was about RE-ENTRY — the body is straight-line
// with no yield point between the write and the read — and it held exactly as long as one OS thread ran every
// green thread. **Two Ms do not need a yield point to be inside one function at once**, so with
// `MAXON_MAX_PROCS>1` two threads writing one word hand each other the other's reading: a wall clock that
// jumps, or a CPU-time bracket that reports another thread's cycles. `SchedRuntime.maxon`'s G2 audit listed
// both as its last outstanding item.
//
// A per-CALL need gets per-CALL storage: `func.stackSize` plus a `StdOp.stackRecordAddr`, which is exactly
// what `GtRuntime.buildGtNowNs` does with the QPC pair and what G2's item 4 did with the other three scratch
// regions. It also deletes two `.data` words, which is why this rung's goldens move for every program that
// reads a clock.
let ClockScratchBytes = 8 as StackSize
let ClockScratchSlot = 0 as SpillSlot

// A FILETIME counts 100-NANOSECOND intervals since 1601-01-01 00:00:00 UTC. Two facts turn that into Unix
// seconds, and both are named rather than spelled inline at the arithmetic:
//   • 10,000,000 ticks per second (100 ns each);
//   • 11,644,473,600 seconds from the 1601 epoch to the 1970 one — 116444736000000000 ticks, which fits an
//     i64 with three orders of magnitude to spare.
let FileTimeTicksPerSecond = 10000000
let FileTimeTicksFrom1601To1970 = 116444736000000000

// ⭐ **DOES THIS CALLEE READ THE WALL CLOCK?** The `__clock_`-only test, the exact twin of
// `isGtRuntimeCallee`. Deliberately NARROW: unlike a `__gt_*` call it implies no scheduler and no heap —
// `__clock_now_unix_s` is a leaf that calls one Win32 import and divides, so a calendar-reading program
// carries neither the allocator nor the leak gate.
export function isClockRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: ClockRuntimeCallPrefix)
end 'isClockRuntimeCallee'

// ⭐ **DOES THIS CALLEE ASK HOW MUCH CPU TIME THIS THREAD HAS USED?** The `__thread_`-only test, the exact
// twin of the wall clock's above and narrow in the same way: `__thread_cpu_ticks` is a leaf that calls one
// Win32 import and loads a word, so a phase-timing program carries neither the allocator nor the scheduler.
export function isThreadCpuRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: ThreadCpuRuntimeCallPrefix)
end 'isThreadCpuRuntimeCallee'

// ⚠ **THIS FILE LAYS OUT NO `.data` AT ALL SINCE W212, and the absence is the point.** Its two words were
// out-param scratch for two host reads, and both are frame slots now (see `ClockScratchSlot`) — so there is
// nothing left for a `clockRuntimeGlobals()` to return, and the producer is gone rather than kept as a
// function that answers an empty array. The `usage` bits it read still gate the two BODIES, in
// `installClockRuntime` below.

// Install the host-clock runtime into the lowered Std module, each entry point on its OWN bit —
// `installProcessRuntime`'s discipline, and here it earns exactly what that file's does: the two entries
// reach two different Win32 imports, and a program almost always wants one of them.
//
// Appended AFTER the pipeline (like every other runtime install), and already-lowered Std so it still
// flows through the whole backend. What runs over that appended band is stated once, in
// `Compiler.compileToCodeResult`'s install block.
export function installClockRuntime(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if usage.usesWallClock 'wallClock'
		buildWallClockNowUnixSeconds(module)
	end 'wallClock'

	if usage.usesThreadCpuTicks 'threadCpu'
		buildThreadCpuTicks(module)
	end 'threadCpu'
end 'installClockRuntime'

// `__clock_now_unix_s() -> i64` — whole seconds since 1970-01-01 UTC. `osReadWallClock` writes
// `GetSystemTimeAsFileTime`'s 100 ns tick count to a `.data` scratch word; the epoch shift and the
// ticks→seconds scale are done HERE in builder-built Std, exactly as `__gt_now_ns` scales QPC ticks — the
// Std tier is where the rewrite's thesis puts clock arithmetic, so the target op stays "read the host's
// calendar into this word" and nothing more.
//
// The subtraction cannot underflow on any clock a program can observe: a FILETIME below the 1970 offset
// means the host's calendar is set before 1970, which Windows does not permit (its own minimum system time
// is 1980). It is a plain i64 `sub`, no guard, for the same reason `__gt_now_ns` needs no 128-bit math.
function buildWallClockNowUnixSeconds(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(WallClockNowUnixSecondsName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")

	// The out-param lives in THIS CALL's frame, not in `.data` — see `ClockScratchSlot`.
	func.stackSize = ClockScratchBytes
	let ticksAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.stackRecordAddr(ticksAddr, slot: ClockScratchSlot))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osReadWallClock(ticksAddr))
	let ticks = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: ticksAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)

	let epochTicks = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: FileTimeTicksFrom1601To1970, ids: ids)
	let sinceEpoch = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: ticks, b: epochTicks, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let perSecond = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: FileTimeTicksPerSecond, ids: ids)
	let seconds = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.div(seconds, lhs: sinceEpoch, rhs: perSecond, signedness: IntegerSignedness.signed))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(seconds))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildWallClockNowUnixSeconds'

// ⭐⭐ **`__thread_cpu_ticks() -> i64`** — the CPU time this thread has consumed, in the host's own unit.
//
// `osThreadCpuTicks` writes `QueryThreadCycleTime`'s tick count into a `.data` scratch word and this loads
// it straight back — `__clock_now_unix_s`'s shape with the arithmetic removed, because there is none to do.
// That absence is the contract rather than an omission: the two platforms answer in units that do not
// convert (TSC ticks vs nanoseconds), so any scaling written here would be the dishonest normalization the
// header refuses. What a caller may do with two readings is SUBTRACT them, and a difference is meaningful
// in either unit.
//
// ⚠ **THE RESULT IS NOT CLAMPED AND MUST NOT BE.** `__cpu_count` clamps because a processor count is a
// DIVISOR for its callers, so a 0 escaping would be a division by zero; a tick count is only ever
// subtracted from a later one. A thread that has just started legitimately reads a very small number, and a
// floor imposed here would make the first bracket of a phase report a cost it did not have.
function buildThreadCpuTicks(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ThreadCpuTicksName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")

	// The out-param lives in THIS CALL's frame, not in `.data` — see `ClockScratchSlot`.
	func.stackSize = ClockScratchBytes
	let ticksAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.stackRecordAddr(ticksAddr, slot: ClockScratchSlot))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osThreadCpuTicks(ticksAddr))
	let ticks = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: ticksAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(ticks))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildThreadCpuTicks'
